The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions on Haiti, including resolutions 2653 (2022), 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023), 2743 (2024), 2751 (2024), 2752 (2024), and 2785 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and unity of Haiti,

Expressing grave concern at the continued deterioration of the security, humanitarian, and human rights situation in Haiti, characterized by the expansion of armed gang violence, criminal acts including kidnappings, homicides, and sexual and gender-based violence, as well as the displacement of hundreds of thousands of civilians,

Commending the ongoing deployment and operations of the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission in Haiti, established pursuant to resolution 2699 (2023) and extended by resolution 2751 (2024), and expressing appreciation to the leadership of Kenya, participating personnel, and contributing Member States for their vital contributions to restoring security in Haiti,

Reiterating the critical importance of coordinated international support to build the capacity of the Haitian National Police (HNP) to counter gangs, restore public safety, secure critical infrastructure, and create security conditions conducive to the holding of free and fair legislative and presidential elections,

Taking note of the requests and appeals from the Haitian authorities for sustained international security assistance, and emphasizing the necessity of a Haitian-owned and Haitian-led political process leading to the restoration of democratic institutions,

Welcoming the Secretary-General’s reporting and recommendations regarding options to support and sustain security and stability in Haiti, and taking note of ongoing discussions regarding the long-term sustainability and financing of international security assistance to Haiti,

Determining that the situation in Haiti continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security and to stability in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the authorization of the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission as set forth in resolution 2699 (2023) and renewed by resolution 2751 (2024) for an additional period of twelve months, until 2 October 2026, to be reviewed after nine months;

2. Reaffirms that the provisions of paragraphs 1 to 21 of resolution 2699 (2023) continue to apply;

3. Calls upon the MSS mission to continue to accelerate its full deployment, expand operational activities in close coordination with the Haitian National Police, and ensure the safety, security, and human rights compliance of all personnel in accordance with international law;

4. Urges Member States to provide additional voluntary financial, logistical, and personnel contributions to the MSS mission, including through the UN Trust Fund established to support the mission, to ensure its full operational capability and sustainability;

5. Encourages the MSS mission and the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH), within their respective mandates, to continue to enhance their coordination and information-sharing to support the Haitian National Police and the political transition;

6. Requests the leadership of the MSS mission, through the Secretary-General, to continue to report to the Security Council every three months on the implementation of its mandate, operational progress, financial status, and human rights compliance;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions on Haiti, including resolutions 2653 (2022), 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023), 2743 (2024), 2751 (2024), 2752 (2024), and 2785 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and unity of Haiti,

Expressing grave concern at the continued deterioration of the security, humanitarian, and human rights situation in Haiti, characterized by the expansion of armed gang violence, criminal acts including kidnappings, homicides, sexual and gender-based violence, grave violations against children, including their recruitment and use by armed gangs, the denial of humanitarian access, as well as the displacement of hundreds of thousands of civilians, and deeply concerned by the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms and ammunition which continues to fuel armed gang violence and destabilize the country,

Commending the ongoing deployment and operations of the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission in Haiti, established pursuant to resolution 2699 (2023) and extended by resolution 2751 (2024), and expressing appreciation to the leadership of Kenya, participating personnel, including personnel and support from CARICOM Member States, and contributing Member States for their vital contributions to restoring security in Haiti,

Reiterating the critical importance of coordinated international support to build the capacity of the Haitian National Police (HNP) to counter gangs, restore public safety, secure critical infrastructure, and create security conditions conducive to the holding of free and fair legislative and presidential elections,

Reiterating the critical importance of strictly enforcing the arms embargo established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) and strengthened by resolution 2752 (2024), and calling upon all Member States to take concrete measures to stem the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms, light weapons, and ammunition into Haiti,

Taking note of the requests and appeals from the Haitian authorities for sustained international security assistance, emphasizing the necessity of an inclusive, Haitian-owned and Haitian-led political process leading to the restoration of democratic institutions, and welcoming the constructive engagement and facilitation efforts of the Caribbean Community (CARICOM) in supporting political dialogue in Haiti,

Taking note of the Secretary-General’s reporting and recommendations regarding options to support and sustain security and stability in Haiti, and taking note of ongoing discussions regarding the long-term sustainability, predictability, and financing of international security assistance to Haiti,

Determining that the situation in Haiti continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security and to stability in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the authorization of the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission as set forth in resolution 2699 (2023) and renewed by resolution 2751 (2024) for an additional period of twelve months, until 2 October 2026, to be reviewed after nine months;

2. Reaffirms that the provisions of paragraphs 1 to 21 of resolution 2699 (2023) continue to apply;

3. Calls upon the MSS mission to continue to accelerate its full deployment, expand operational activities in close coordination with the Haitian National Police, ensure adequate linguistic capabilities in French and Haitian Creole, and ensure the safety, security, and human rights compliance of all personnel in accordance with international law, including child protection measures and robust mechanisms to prevent and respond to sexual exploitation and abuse, and calls upon all Haitian political stakeholders to urgently advance an inclusive, Haitian-led and Haitian-owned political dialogue to establish a clear and credible roadmap for the holding of free and fair elections;

4. Reiterates its demand that Member States strictly implement the arms embargo and take all necessary measures to prevent the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms, light weapons, and ammunition to armed gangs and non-state actors in Haiti, in full compliance with resolution 2653 (2022) and resolution 2752 (2024);

5. Urges Member States to provide additional voluntary financial, logistical, and personnel contributions to the MSS mission, including through the UN Trust Fund established to support the mission and in cooperation with regional and international partners such as the International Organization of la Francophonie (OIF), notes with concern the funding shortfalls facing the mission, emphasizes the need for predictable and sustainable support to ensure its full operational capability, and calls for increased humanitarian assistance to address the acute needs of the affected population;

6. Encourages the MSS mission and the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH), within their respective mandates, to continue to enhance their coordination and information-sharing to support the Haitian National Police and the political transition;

7. Requests the leadership of the MSS mission, through the Secretary-General, to continue to report to the Security Council every three months on the implementation of its mandate, operational progress, financial status, and human rights compliance;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

